.bd
FILE
.in +5
.br
.bd
disklabel
- format of the Micronix disk label
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
Every Micronix disk describes itself in a label that a driver can read
without being told anything about the drive beforehand.  It lives in the
second half of the boot sector - physical cylinder 0, head 0, sector 0,
the one sector that needs no geometry to reach - beginning at byte 256
(DL_OFFSET), because the first half holds the boot code.
.sp
The label is the drive speaking, not the controller: it carries the
geometry and the timing, because both are properties of the drive, and
it carries a table of eight slices that say where the filesystems and
the swap live.  One format is shared by every block driver.  A drive
with no label has no geometry, and the only operations possible on it
are labelling and formatting, through its whole-disk slice
.bd
c
.
.sp
The fields, in the order they occupy the label:
.nf
.bd 9
struct dlabel {
    char  d_magic[4];       "MWDL", the magic number
    UINT  d_version;        the label format's version

    UINT  d_tracks;         cylinders
    UINT  d_heads;          heads
    UINT  d_spt;            512-byte blocks per track
    UINT  d_firstsec;       first sector number on a track

    UINT8 d_step;           step pulse delay, 100 us
    UINT8 d_seek;           head settle after a seek, 100 us
    UINT  d_precomp;        cylinder where write precomp begins;
                            0xffff = never
    UINT  d_lowcur;         cylinder where low write current begins;
                            0xffff = never

    UINT  d_bootblks;       blocks the boot owns from device block 0;
                            0 = none
    UINT  d_bootslice;      the slice the boot loads from

    struct dslice d_slice[8];       a through h

    char  d_mfg[16];        the drive's manufacturer
    char  d_model[16];      its model number
};
.fi
.sp
The timing fields are properties of the drive.  A drive with its own
controller - IDE, SCSI - reads them and ignores them; the MFM drivers
use them to step and to command the write current.
.sp
Each slice is:
.nf
.bd 9
struct dslice {
    UINT  d_off;            where it starts, in cylinders
    UINT  d_len;            how many cylinders, 0 = not carved
    UINT  d_roll;           rotation within the slice, 0 = none
    UINT8 d_type;           what the slice is for
};
.fi
.sp
d_type is 0 for unused, 1 for a version 6 filesystem, and 2 for a swap
area.  Slice
.bd
c
- the third entry - is always unused: it is the whole disk, from
cylinder 0, and its extent is not the table's to give, so its entry is
never consulted.
.sp
A slice's offset is in cylinders, not blocks, so a slice's position on a
large drive never has to fit in a 16-bit block number.  A slice may roll
its own contents within its extent, so a filesystem can put its inode
table in the middle of the platter rather than at the edge; zero, the
ordinary case, is no rotation.  A length of zero means the slice has not
been carved, and the driver refuses to map it.
.sp
Block 0 is never part of a filesystem: every slice reserves it, so the
label sits outside every filesystem on the disk and cannot be destroyed
by making one.  The formatter writes a prototype label - the geometry,
the timing, and the conventional slice types, a, d, e, f, g and h as
filesystems and b as swap - so a freshly formatted disk says what it is
and how it is meant to be partitioned.
.sp 2
.in -5
